+## Obligations for Dependent Nodes
 
-Retrieval must distinguish a usable current state from unresolved review work. Exactly how those states are represented remains part of the open choice.
+- **tablet-sync** stores, per local visit copy, the revision ID it was edited from; generates revision IDs; submits queued edits of one visit oldest first; and explains an `unresolved` result to the worker using the competing revisions returned.
+- **review-export** builds its coordinator work from the unresolved-visit listing and competing revisions, submits resolutions naming exactly the revisions the coordinator compared, and handles the "competing revisions changed" rejection by showing the coordinator the new set. An unresolved visit has no current revision to export.
 
-## Open Design Choice: Concurrent Visit Edits
+## Decisions & Alternatives
 
-OPEN: select the concurrency policy before completing this node's public contract.
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|----------|--------|------------------------|-----------|
+| Concurrency policy | Whole-visit revisions: concurrent edits compete as complete visits and a coordinator resolves them | Field-aware three-way merge: disjoint field changes combine automatically, overlapping changes go to review | Automatic merging needs maintained rules for scalar fields, observer lists, and species entries, and can combine edits that are scientifically related (a species identification and its cover estimate) without anyone noticing. A larger review queue is the accepted cost: any two edits made from the same revision create coordinator work, including edits to unrelated fields, and that visit has no current revision until resolved. |
+| Who assigns revision IDs | The submitting client | Server-assigned IDs returned on acceptance | A tablet offline for 48 hours must be able to name the base of a second edit before the first has been acknowledged. |
+| Idempotency key | The revision ID | A separate key alongside the revision ID | One client-generated identifier per submission already exists; a second would let the two disagree. |
+| Staleness of a worker's edit | Always accepted; competes with whatever replaced its base | Reject edits whose base is superseded and make the tablet rebase | Rejection pushes merge work onto a field worker and risks the edit never being resubmitted, against the tenet of not silently discarding a submitted edit. |
+| Edit whose base the store has not received | Rejected until the base is accepted | Hold the edit until its base arrives | A held edit is in neither the tablet's failure state nor the visit's history; rejecting keeps it with the tablet, which already retries. |
+| What a resolution contains | A complete visit payload authored by the coordinator | Choosing one competing revision only | Choosing one would drop the other worker's edit even when the two are unrelated, forcing a second correction; a coordinator-authored visit keeps a person responsible for the combination. |
+| Resolution against changed competitors | Rejected, with the new set returned | Accept and leave the unnamed revision competing with the resolution | Coordinators work online, so a retry is cheap, and it prevents a resolution being recorded by someone who never saw one of the competitors. |
+| Identical competing payloads | Still compete | Settle automatically when payloads match | The store compares no payload contents anywhere else; one exception is the start of merge rules. |
 
-Two approaches remain credible:
+## Open Questions & Future Decisions
 
-- Whole-visit revisions: each edit names its starting revision. Concurrent edits produce competing complete revisions for a coordinator to resolve. This preserves the worker's submitted visit as a unit and needs relatively little merge logic, but even edits to unrelated fields create review work. Tablet-sync would retain base revision IDs and display pending competing revisions. Review-export would need a revision comparison workflow and exclude unresolved visits from its export set.
-- Field-aware three-way merge: each edit supplies its base state and changes. Disjoint field changes combine automatically; overlapping changes require review. This reduces review volume and gets more visits ready for export, but requires maintained rules for scalar fields, observer lists, and species entries. Automatically combined fields can also cross scientific relationships the rules do not understand. Tablet-sync would retain base snapshots and send structured changes. Review-export would need field-level provenance and a review view for overlapping changes.
+### Deferred
 
-The choice trades review workload and submission size against implementation burden and preservation of whole-visit intent. Neither approach may discard original submissions. Field-aware merging is not ruled out by a requirement that every combined record receive manual approval.
+1. Whether current-state retrieval should name a comparison baseline (the revision the competing revisions diverged from) is decided with review-export; revision history already exposes every revision's bases.
+2. How the coordinator role is conveyed by the team's identity provider.
+3. Two workers who independently create visits for the same plot and date produce two visits with different IDs. These are duplicates rather than competing revisions, and nothing here detects them.
+4. Withdrawing or deleting a visit.
 
